WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.759
Every secret has a lifecycle: creation, storage, use, rotation, expiration, revocation, replacement, and retirement.

2
00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:17.525
If a team only thinks about the moment a value is created, the secret can become permanent infrastructure that nobody knows how to replace safely.

3
00:00:17.525 --> 00:00:20.736
Every secret should have an owner and a purpose.

4
00:00:20.736 --> 00:00:29.900
Ownership answers who approves access, who rotates the value, who responds to suspected exposure, and who decides when it can be retired.

5
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.983
Purpose answers why the secret exists and which systems should depend on it.

6
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:40.802
Rotation schedules should reflect risk, system capability, and operational constraints.

7
00:00:40.802 --> 00:00:43.077
Some secrets rotate automatically.

8
00:00:43.077 --> 00:00:50.501
Others require coordinated application deployment, downstream notification, or staged acceptance of two values.

9
00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:56.455
The team should understand the rotation model before an emergency forces a rushed change.

10
00:00:56.455 --> 00:01:00.669
Staged rollout patterns can reduce downtime during replacement.

11
00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:11.103
At a high level, a system may temporarily accept an old and new value while clients move over, or it may deploy a new reference before revoking the old one.

12
00:01:11.103 --> 00:01:19.866
The exact pattern depends on the platform and integration, but the principle is planned transition instead of surprise replacement.

13
00:01:19.866 --> 00:01:25.150
Emergency revocation after suspected exposure should be rehearsed conceptually.

14
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:39.866
Teams should know how to contain use, rotate or revoke the value, redeploy affected services, update dependent systems, monitor for failures, and communicate to stakeholders without exposing additional sensitive details.

15
00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:49.096
Old credentials must be removed from applications, pipelines, secret stores, documentation, scripts, support notes, and dependent systems.

16
00:01:49.096 --> 00:01:52.642
Retired secrets should not linger as fallback values.

17
00:01:52.642 --> 00:01:58.327
Permanent unmanaged credentials are a sign that lifecycle management has broken down.
